  "transcript": "User: Hi — I saw your ad on Facebook about answering missed calls. I keep losing patients after hours, so I'm curious how it works.\nAgent: Got it, thanks for telling me that — losing patients after hours hurts.  \n\nAt a high level, we set up a system so when your office doesn’t pick up (after-hours or when you’re tied up), the call is still answered, the patient is helped, and you don’t lose that visit.  \n\nBefore I go into the how, can you walk me through what happens now when someone calls after hours — does it ring out, go to voicemail, or to an answering service?\nUser: Currently, calls go straight to voicemail after hours, and we get about 20 of those each week.\nAgent: Yeah, that makes sense — if they hit voicemail, a lot of them won’t wait.  \n\nThose 20 after-hours calls… do people usually leave a message, or is it more that they hang up and probably call another practice?\nUser: Most hang up and likely call another practice. I believe half would book if someone answered.\nAgent: Mhm, I hear you — if they’re in pain or stressed, they won’t wait around for voicemail.  \n\nHas anyone ever walked you through what losing those after-hours calls actually costs you each week?\nUser: No, I haven't had anyone walk me through it, but I know I'm losing potential bookings.\nAgent: Makes sense — you can feel it, even without the math.  \n\nSo, ballpark, what’s an average visit worth for you in dollars when one of those callers actually books — a typical new-patient appointment or treatment?\nUser: An average appointment is worth about $650 to my dental practice, Bright Smile Dental.\nAgent: Got it, thanks for sharing that.  \n\nSo with your numbers at Bright Smile — 20 after-hours calls a week, about half would book, and each is roughly $650 — that’s around $6,500 a week walking out the door… and over a month, about $28,100. Does that feel in the ballpark of what you’re seeing, or does it surprise you?\nUser: Yes, that sounds about right. It's frustrating to see so much potential revenue slip away each month.\nAgent: Yeah, I hear you — for a dental practice, that’s a lot of lost treatment and emergency work every single month.  \n\nSo… to stop that, we do a simple thing: when your team can’t answer (after hours or when you’re with a patient), calls get picked up, the patient is handled like a real person, and qualified ones are booked straight into your schedule or sent to your team with all the details — so those $650 visits don’t disappear.  \n\nTo really show you what that looks like for Bright Smile, it’s a quick 20‑minute walkthrough where we plug in your numbers and call flows; what’s better for you for that — later this week or early next week?\nUser: Let's book the live call for tomorrow afternoon. Can you offer specific times?\nAgent: Love it, tomorrow afternoon works.  \n\nFirst, I just need a couple details so we book this right — what’s your first name?\nUser: My first name is Maria.\nAgent: Got it, Maria, thanks.  \n\nAnd what’s your last name?\nUser: My last name is Gonzales.\nAgent: Perfect, Maria Gonzales, thank you.  \n\nQuick check — you’re on Central time, right?\nUser: Yes, that's correct. I'm in the Central time zone, here in Chicago.\nAgent: Perfect, thanks for confirming Central.  \n\nTomorrow afternoon I can get you in as soon as 1:00 or 1:45 Central — which works better for you for the demo?\nUser: 1:45 Central works for me. Yes, please book the live call for that time.\nAgent: One moment while I confirm that for you.\nAgent: Maria, you’re all set: tomorrow at 1:45 Central with Jay for your Bright Smile Dental call. You’ll get a text message in a moment with the confirmation details so you can add it to your calendar or reschedule if something comes up.\n\nIs there anything else I can help you with right now?\n\nOkay, perfect. Really glad you called in today — Jay’s looking forward to walking through how to stop those after-hours losses. Have a great rest of your day. Bye!",
